WEBVTT

1
00:00:16.809 --> 00:00:28.809
<font color="#ec14bd">Credits & Thanks to Dahom for the subtitle.</font>

2
00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:41.810
<font color="#ffff80"><b>Fixed & Synced by bozxphd. Enjoy The Flick</b>.</font>

3
00:00:42.811 --> 00:00:44.933
On the house.

4
00:00:45.013 --> 00:00:47.736
Welcome back, Dash! Hiya, Sam.

5
00:00:47.816 --> 00:00:51.840
Hey, uh, we've got a real,
live rock star in the house!

6
00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:52.508
Where?

7
00:00:52.588 --> 00:00:55.243
Hey...

8
00:00:55.323 --> 00:00:57.479
- Thanks, Freddie! Cheers!
- Cheers.

9
00:00:57.559 --> 00:00:59.481
You must be happy to
have this fool back.

10
00:00:59.561 --> 00:01:02.016
Mm! You have no idea, Freddie.

11
00:01:02.096 --> 00:01:04.886
You know, at a certain
point, I need new stories

12
00:01:04.966 --> 00:01:07.422
to continue living
vicariously through him.

13
00:01:07.502 --> 00:01:09.491
- Oh...
- Hello.

14
00:01:09.571 --> 00:01:12.293
Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.
Every day a new city,

15
00:01:12.373 --> 00:01:17.198
new ladies, but
that gets tiring.

16
00:01:17.278 --> 00:01:19.400
- Tiring?
- So tiring. This poor guy!

17
00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:22.904
Okay. Okay, you're right.
It's awesome.

18
00:01:22.984 --> 00:01:24.038
I hate you.

19
00:01:24.118 --> 00:01:26.908
But know what I want
more than anything?

20
00:01:26.988 --> 00:01:28.023
What?

21
00:01:29.357 --> 00:01:31.980
- To dig up worms...
- Oh, man!

22
00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:37.285
And go fishing with my
brother like when we were kids!

23
00:01:37.365 --> 00:01:41.056
I'm going fishing
with my brother!

24
00:01:41.136 --> 00:01:42.405
Okay.

25
00:01:43.305 --> 00:01:44.927
Another round, Freddie!

26
00:01:45.007 --> 00:01:46.209
- Whew.
- Whoo!

27
00:01:51.614 --> 00:01:53.335
- Oh, man, those drums.
- This one's for you!

28
00:01:53.415 --> 00:01:56.939
We're in... Just look at all the people.
Sing, go. Go.

29
00:01:57.019 --> 00:01:58.808
♪ You have to do it running ♪

30
00:01:58.888 --> 00:02:02.211
♪ But you do everything
that they ask you to ♪

31
00:02:02.291 --> 00:02:06.348
♪ 'Cause you don't mind seeing
yourself in a picture ♪

32
00:02:06.428 --> 00:02:09.418
♪ As long as you look far away ♪

33
00:02:09.498 --> 00:02:10.886
♪ As long as you look removed... ♪

34
00:02:10.966 --> 00:02:12.855
This is the part I know.
This is the part I know!

35
00:02:12.935 --> 00:02:15.357
- ♪ Showered and blue-blazered...
♪ -Now you got it!

36
00:02:15.437 --> 00:02:18.828
- ♪ Fill yourself with quarters... ♪
- What does that mean?

37
00:02:18.908 --> 00:02:22.331
- I don't know.
- ♪ Showered and blue-blazered ♪

38
00:02:22.411 --> 00:02:25.935
♪ You get mistaken for strangers
by your own friends ♪

39
00:02:26.015 --> 00:02:27.837
♪ When you pass them at night ♪

40
00:02:27.917 --> 00:02:31.373
♪ Under the silvery,
silvery Citibank lights ♪

41
00:02:31.453 --> 00:02:36.212
♪ Arm in arm in arm and eyes
and eyes glazing over ♪

42
00:02:36.292 --> 00:02:38.881
-♪ Under ♪ -♪ Under ♪

43
00:02:38.961 --> 00:02:40.583
Under?

44
00:02:40.663 --> 00:02:44.019
- Over, over!
- No, it doesn't mean that.

45
00:02:44.099 --> 00:02:47.991
♪ Another un-innocent,
elegant fall ♪

46
00:02:48.071 --> 00:02:51.261
♪ Into the un-magnificent
lives of adults ♪

47
00:02:51.341 --> 00:02:54.998
- ♪ Showered and
blue-blazered ♪ -

48
00:02:55.078 --> 00:02:57.381
♪ Fill yourself with quarters ♪

49
00:03:25.175 --> 00:03:26.210
Dash...

50
00:03:29.079 --> 00:03:31.034
Oh, no.

51
00:03:31.114 --> 00:03:33.017
Oh, my God.

52
00:03:34.384 --> 00:03:36.020
Oh, my God.

53
00:03:39.389 --> 00:03:40.591
You're pinned.

54
00:03:53.270 --> 00:03:58.076
What are you... What
are you saying, Dash?

55
00:04:02.013 --> 00:04:05.150
I-I-I can't understand
what you're saying.

56
00:04:12.289 --> 00:04:14.159
Somebody help me!

57
00:04:15.226 --> 00:04:17.048
Please!

58
00:04:17.128 --> 00:04:18.529
Help me!

59
00:04:47.625 --> 00:04:49.194
Good morning.

60
00:04:53.664 --> 00:04:56.067
Why don't I put you up here.

61
00:04:57.068 --> 00:04:58.703
That's a good view.

62
00:05:01.206 --> 00:05:02.775
There you go.

63
00:05:34.239 --> 00:05:38.663
Memory is the ultimate definer

64
00:05:38.743 --> 00:05:40.379
of our lives.

65
00:05:42.447 --> 00:05:46.652
For what are our lives, but
a collection of memories...

66
00:05:48.253 --> 00:05:53.793
memories of events,
experiences, emotions...

67
00:05:55.260 --> 00:05:58.150
all stamped onto our
nervous systems,

68
00:05:58.230 --> 00:06:00.585
all leading to here and now,

69
00:06:00.665 --> 00:06:04.270
making us each the
person we are today.

70
00:06:07.573 --> 00:06:12.813
Without them, the present
would be void of context.

71
00:06:19.352 --> 00:06:20.787
And complete.

72
00:07:04.397 --> 00:07:07.253
Now, I want you to
close your eyes

73
00:07:07.333 --> 00:07:10.556
and think of a memory you
associate with your youth.

74
00:07:10.636 --> 00:07:12.558
Happy birthday!

75
00:07:12.638 --> 00:07:16.276
Just let it come, unfiltered.

76
00:07:21.448 --> 00:07:23.751
Now think of a memory
that makes you happy.

77
00:07:28.755 --> 00:07:33.261
Now consider the most painful
memory of your life.

78
00:07:34.795 --> 00:07:36.531
Now open your eyes.

79
00:07:38.999 --> 00:07:41.269
The moments of your lives...

80
00:07:42.436 --> 00:07:43.905
that make a life...

81
00:07:44.771 --> 00:07:46.541
that define you.

82
00:07:48.575 --> 00:07:52.266
We tend to think of memories
as these filed-away facts

83
00:07:52.346 --> 00:07:54.501
stored safely in the
brain, when in actuality,

84
00:07:54.581 --> 00:07:56.803
everything that's
happened to you

85
00:07:56.883 --> 00:08:00.307
since the event you remember

86
00:08:00.387 --> 00:08:03.443
has altered the
memory of that event,

87
00:08:03.523 --> 00:08:07.948
providing a new filter through
which you experience it,

88
00:08:08.028 --> 00:08:11.852
a filter that
changes, and forgets,

89
00:08:11.932 --> 00:08:16.356
and exaggerates, and
combines, and morphs.

90
00:08:16.436 --> 00:08:21.329
But the pure experience does
still exist within you,

91
00:08:21.409 --> 00:08:23.631
for your brain has the ability

92
00:08:23.711 --> 00:08:28.203
to remember every event
that's ever happened to you,

93
00:08:28.283 --> 00:08:30.419
down to the greatest of detail.

94
00:08:31.586 --> 00:08:35.310
The problem is you have
no way of recalling it,

95
00:08:35.390 --> 00:08:38.613
no way to clear the
filters of time

96
00:08:38.693 --> 00:08:41.230
and get back to the truth.

97
00:08:42.430 --> 00:08:44.219
Until now.

98
00:08:44.299 --> 00:08:47.455
The Rememory machine
allows people

99
00:08:47.535 --> 00:08:51.940
to experience the
truth of their lives.

100
00:08:55.777 --> 00:08:57.365
What you're seeing before you

101
00:08:57.445 --> 00:09:02.351
are actual extracted memories
from our test group.

102
00:09:31.580 --> 00:09:34.336
Mom! Mom!

103
00:09:34.416 --> 00:09:36.886
Good morning, birthday boy.

104
00:09:39.121 --> 00:09:40.890
Please.

105
00:09:56.605 --> 00:10:02.765
Quite simply, this device allows people
to experience the real event again

106
00:10:02.845 --> 00:10:04.900
by extracting and transcribing

107
00:10:04.980 --> 00:10:07.836
the actual memory
from their brain

108
00:10:07.916 --> 00:10:10.406
and displaying it before them,

109
00:10:10.486 --> 00:10:12.741
providing the opportunity
to understand

110
00:10:12.821 --> 00:10:16.445
and, more importantly,
cope with their past.

111
00:10:16.525 --> 00:10:22.699
Pure, uncluttered,
unfiltered truth.

112
00:10:24.066 --> 00:10:25.901
Thank you very much for coming.

113
00:10:36.579 --> 00:10:38.635
Brilliant! Absolutely brilliant!

114
00:10:38.715 --> 00:10:40.503
You tapped into the
personal, Gordon,

115
00:10:40.583 --> 00:10:41.905
and that is how you
change the world.

116
00:10:41.985 --> 00:10:43.873
- We need more time.
- But why?

117
00:10:43.953 --> 00:10:45.241
You've done what
you set out to do.

118
00:10:45.321 --> 00:10:48.645
After what Neil just disclosed minutes
before I had to go out there!

119
00:10:48.725 --> 00:10:50.814
He shouldn't have put such
a concern before you.

120
00:10:50.894 --> 00:10:52.015
I have a right to know!

121
00:10:52.095 --> 00:10:54.050
It in no way changes
the science.

122
00:10:54.130 --> 00:10:56.820
Then why did you
keep it from me?

123
00:10:56.900 --> 00:10:59.570
I wish you'd see that
I was protecting you.

124
00:11:03.039 --> 00:11:04.527
I'll tie up all the loose ends.

125
00:11:04.607 --> 00:11:07.364
All the research indicates
the machine is safe.

126
00:11:07.444 --> 00:11:10.367
I want more time. Those
patients are my responsibility.

127
00:11:10.447 --> 00:11:12.769
Look, Gordon, you are
a brilliant man.

128
00:11:12.849 --> 00:11:14.771
You've developed
something extraordinary,

129
00:11:14.851 --> 00:11:17.607
something that will rival the
world's greatest inventions.

130
00:11:17.687 --> 00:11:19.743
It will perfect the legal
system, cure Alzheimer's,

131
00:11:19.823 --> 00:11:24.428
but more importantly,
it'll help people heal.

132
00:11:25.662 --> 00:11:28.585
Focus on the good it'll bring.

133
00:11:28.665 --> 00:11:29.733
Be proud.

134
00:11:31.968 --> 00:11:34.438
That prototype is our future.

135
00:11:42.112 --> 00:11:44.068
Hi, thank you. Thank
you for coming.

136
00:11:44.148 --> 00:11:45.403
Thank you for coming.

137
00:11:45.483 --> 00:11:47.538
- Gordon?
- Not here, Wendy.

138
00:11:47.618 --> 00:11:49.120
I want them back.

139
00:11:51.522 --> 00:11:55.079
They're not here. They're
locked away at my house.

140
00:11:55.159 --> 00:11:59.183
Well, get them. Gordon,
you have no right...

141
00:11:59.263 --> 00:12:00.418
Ahem.

142
00:12:00.498 --> 00:12:03.487
To project my past in front
of people I don't even know.

143
00:12:03.567 --> 00:12:05.423
You knew exactly
what this entailed,

144
00:12:05.503 --> 00:12:07.525
and you know they're needed
to verify the research.

145
00:12:07.605 --> 00:12:11.128
I didn't know what you'd find.

146
00:12:11.208 --> 00:12:14.698
And that you'd use them
to manipulate me...

147
00:12:14.778 --> 00:12:16.967
You know that's not
what happened.

148
00:12:17.047 --> 00:12:18.750
What happened, Gordon?

149
00:12:20.050 --> 00:12:22.039
What happened?

150
00:12:22.119 --> 00:12:24.556
Gordon, please, just tell me.

151
00:12:32.196 --> 00:12:36.234
Wendy... I'm sorry.

152
00:13:51.610 --> 00:13:52.964
- Here she comes.
- Here she comes.

153
00:13:53.044 --> 00:13:55.134
Let's take a picture.

154
00:13:55.214 --> 00:13:56.202
Is it picture time?

155
00:13:56.282 --> 00:13:57.770
- Get... Get ready!
- Okay!

156
00:13:57.850 --> 00:14:00.540
This is gotta be good,
don't mess it up.

157
00:14:00.620 --> 00:14:02.108
- Make sure you frame us!
- Get together!

158
00:14:02.188 --> 00:14:06.212
- Okay, we're together.
- Don't forget to smile. Say cheese!

159
00:14:06.292 --> 00:14:08.161
Cheese!

160
00:14:10.663 --> 00:14:13.900
Daddy, come on! Let's
go build a sandcastle!

161
00:14:16.202 --> 00:14:20.340
I can't think of a time in my
life when I've been happier.

162
00:14:23.909 --> 00:14:25.731
Me neither.

163
00:14:25.811 --> 00:14:29.068
Everything is in
its right place.

164
00:14:29.148 --> 00:14:33.906
Gordon. Gordon.

165
00:14:33.986 --> 00:14:35.188
Gordon!

166
00:14:41.794 --> 00:14:43.630
Todd, what are you doing here?

167
00:14:44.330 --> 00:14:47.053
- We should talk.
- Uhh...

168
00:14:47.133 --> 00:14:50.723
This isn't entirely appropriate.
What can I do for you?

169
00:14:50.803 --> 00:14:53.960
- You brought things back...
- To help you work through them.

170
00:14:54.040 --> 00:14:58.998
Things that should've
stayed buried in the past.

171
00:14:59.078 --> 00:15:02.669
It's a process, Todd. It can be difficult.
I've explained that.

172
00:15:02.749 --> 00:15:06.673
- You fucked up my head!
- No. No, I didn't, Todd.

173
00:15:06.753 --> 00:15:10.611
You came to me hurt and injured.
We're moving forward...

174
00:15:10.691 --> 00:15:13.880
No. No, you...

175
00:15:13.960 --> 00:15:16.683
You came to me.

176
00:15:16.763 --> 00:15:21.402
My life? My life
was better before.

177
00:15:28.975 --> 00:15:31.164
You ruined my life,

178
00:15:31.244 --> 00:15:34.901
and now you're gonna
make a bunch of money?

179
00:15:34.981 --> 00:15:37.852
Todd. Todd, listen to me.

180
00:15:38.919 --> 00:15:40.254
Jesus, Todd!

181
00:16:36.244 --> 00:16:40.234
I can't think of a time in my
life when I've been happier.

182
00:16:40.314 --> 00:16:42.236
Me neither.

183
00:16:42.316 --> 00:16:46.254
Everything in its right place.

184
00:18:44.307 --> 00:18:46.129
No bullet wounds.

185
00:18:46.209 --> 00:18:49.199
Yes, but those are bullet
holes, aren't they?

186
00:18:49.279 --> 00:18:50.567
Mm-hmm.

187
00:18:50.647 --> 00:18:53.183
Well, they weren't
there yesterday.

188
00:18:55.118 --> 00:18:58.208
Any recent arguments,
disagreements between Mr. Dunn

189
00:18:58.288 --> 00:18:59.976
and anyone here at the company?

190
00:19:00.056 --> 00:19:04.161
No, no. None that I'm aware of.

191
00:19:07.530 --> 00:19:09.118
Gonna need to see the
security footage,

192
00:19:09.198 --> 00:19:11.635
names of all the people with
access to this building.

193
00:19:12.669 --> 00:19:14.037
Yes, of course.

194
00:19:20.376 --> 00:19:23.180
Come on, that's a good girl.

195
00:19:27.250 --> 00:19:29.052
Carolyn.

196
00:19:36.627 --> 00:19:38.415
Gordon Dunn was not only

197
00:19:38.495 --> 00:19:39.950
a profoundly brilliant man,

198
00:19:40.030 --> 00:19:41.652
but a profoundly good man,

199
00:19:41.732 --> 00:19:44.355
a rare combination
in my experience,

200
00:19:44.435 --> 00:19:46.056
and one that made him

201
00:19:46.136 --> 00:19:49.326
an extraordinary
person to be around.

202
00:19:49.406 --> 00:19:51.242
I count myself lucky...

203
00:19:54.044 --> 00:19:57.215
I count myself fortunate...

204
00:20:00.351 --> 00:20:03.655
for every moment I was able
to spend in his company.

205
00:20:05.456 --> 00:20:08.178
This is a terrible loss.

206
00:20:08.258 --> 00:20:13.550
He was a true pioneer, and
his work will live on.

207
00:20:13.630 --> 00:20:15.619
We will mourn him
deeply at Cortex

208
00:20:15.699 --> 00:20:19.556
as we begin to recover
from the shock.

209
00:20:19.636 --> 00:20:23.027
This is not just a Cortex loss,

210
00:20:23.107 --> 00:20:25.543
this is the world's loss.

211
00:20:27.011 --> 00:20:29.199
Why have the police
opened an investigation?

212
00:20:29.279 --> 00:20:32.002
While we have no doubt that
he died of natural causes,

213
00:20:32.082 --> 00:20:33.604
we simply want to
be beyond certain

214
00:20:33.684 --> 00:20:35.941
that nothing
unscrupulous took place,

215
00:20:36.021 --> 00:20:37.609
given the unfortunate timing.

216
00:20:37.689 --> 00:20:41.046
But rest assured,
it will not delay

217
00:20:41.126 --> 00:20:43.448
or alter the production
of the machine.

218
00:20:43.528 --> 00:20:44.630
Thank you.

219
00:21:02.580 --> 00:21:04.469
Neil, what do you have?

220
00:21:04.549 --> 00:21:07.739
- Well, you're not gonna like it.
- Surprise me.

221
00:21:07.819 --> 00:21:10.108
Well, as you know, the final
stages of the Rememory machine

222
00:21:10.188 --> 00:21:11.676
were completed almost entirely

223
00:21:11.756 --> 00:21:13.445
by Gordon himself,
the essential steps.

224
00:21:13.525 --> 00:21:16.614
Yes, but he must've left behind
extensive notes, plans...

225
00:21:16.694 --> 00:21:19.184
You know how secretive
and protective he was.

226
00:21:19.264 --> 00:21:21.453
That's why he demanded using
actual glass for the memories

227
00:21:21.533 --> 00:21:23.488
instead of storing them
on a closed cloud server.

228
00:21:23.568 --> 00:21:26.291
We're in his network, but
all his data is encrypted,

229
00:21:26.371 --> 00:21:27.559
and sophisticatedly so.

230
00:21:27.639 --> 00:21:29.294
I mean, it's actually
quite brilliant...

231
00:21:29.374 --> 00:21:33.631
Are you telling me that
we can't reverse engineer

232
00:21:33.711 --> 00:21:35.633
the product that we engineered?

233
00:21:35.713 --> 00:21:39.218
Not without Gordon's prototype,
at least not for some time.

234
00:21:41.387 --> 00:21:43.776
Define "some time."

235
00:21:43.856 --> 00:21:47.127
A year? Optimistically.

236
00:21:51.230 --> 00:21:56.102
Well. Well, it
can't just be lost.

237
00:22:04.310 --> 00:22:06.179
Thank you. Bye.

238
00:22:28.634 --> 00:22:30.356
I'm testing you.

239
00:22:30.436 --> 00:22:33.159
- I don't think you remember.
- The sea lion.

240
00:22:33.239 --> 00:22:35.494
Yes, yes!

241
00:22:35.574 --> 00:22:37.063
Weeks after we showed them,

242
00:22:37.143 --> 00:22:39.665
we showed them numbers and
symbols on the cards,

243
00:22:39.745 --> 00:22:41.167
and then weeks after,

244
00:22:41.247 --> 00:22:43.402
they could correctly
identify the matching cards.

245
00:22:43.482 --> 00:22:47.106
That's amazing. Their brains are
a quarter the size of ours.

246
00:22:47.186 --> 00:22:49.776
Mm. So that alone is
advanced cognitive function,

247
00:22:49.856 --> 00:22:52.679
but you know what was
really incredible?

248
00:22:52.759 --> 00:22:54.247
Hmm, what?

249
00:22:54.327 --> 00:22:56.850
They could remember the whole
thing ten years later.

250
00:22:56.930 --> 00:22:58.785
People can't even do that.

251
00:22:58.865 --> 00:23:00.835
Some people in particular!

252
00:23:16.449 --> 00:23:17.818
- Hi.
- Hello.

253
00:23:18.718 --> 00:23:20.474
- Hi.
- Um. Sorry.

254
00:23:20.554 --> 00:23:23.376
I'm James Klinch. I'm
here for the reception.

255
00:23:23.456 --> 00:23:25.645
Yes, I'm sorry, I
sent everyone away.

256
00:23:25.725 --> 00:23:30.784
I just, I couldn't take any more
mindless platitudes. I'm sorry.

257
00:23:30.864 --> 00:23:32.366
Thanks for coming.

258
00:23:51.585 --> 00:23:53.340
Time heals all wounds.

259
00:23:53.420 --> 00:23:57.411
Uh... that's a really bad joke.

260
00:23:57.491 --> 00:23:59.147
The platitudes...

261
00:23:59.227 --> 00:24:01.349
It's quite cheeky under
the circumstances, yeah.

262
00:24:01.429 --> 00:24:05.153
I know you want to be alone,
but I just wanted to say

263
00:24:05.233 --> 00:24:08.289
how truly sorry I am. Truly.

264
00:24:08.369 --> 00:24:09.404
Thank you.

265
00:24:10.905 --> 00:24:12.507
How did you know Gordon?

266
00:24:15.376 --> 00:24:16.811
He saved my life.

267
00:24:18.479 --> 00:24:21.449
Now, that's a story
I'd like to hear.

268
00:24:23.718 --> 00:24:25.187
Thank you.

269
00:24:30.658 --> 00:24:32.260
You can help me clean up.

270
00:24:41.535 --> 00:24:45.193
We happened to be staying at
the same hotel one night.

271
00:24:45.273 --> 00:24:49.864
He was there for a conference,
and I was there...

272
00:24:49.944 --> 00:24:53.901
because my life
hadn't worked out,

273
00:24:53.981 --> 00:24:57.219
not the way I imagined,
and I was in a bad place.

274
00:24:58.653 --> 00:25:01.957
And the hotel was a
very tall building.

275
00:25:04.593 --> 00:25:06.915
There was a terrible
storm that night,

276
00:25:06.995 --> 00:25:08.483
but it was strangely warm.

277
00:25:08.563 --> 00:25:10.633
It felt like a
hurricane was coming.

278
00:25:12.334 --> 00:25:14.823
I couldn't sleep, so I went
down to the hotel bar.

279
00:25:14.903 --> 00:25:16.692
It was a typical hotel bar,

280
00:25:16.772 --> 00:25:18.393
which is to say

281
00:25:18.473 --> 00:25:22.264
it's really the only thing
I can remember about it.

282
00:25:22.344 --> 00:25:25.448
But Gordon was sitting
there, having a nightcap.

283
00:25:26.982 --> 00:25:29.252
He seemed lost in thought.

284
00:25:29.951 --> 00:25:33.022
I was... drowning in mine.

285
00:25:35.324 --> 00:25:37.879
After a few moments, he
turns to me and he says,

286
00:25:37.959 --> 00:25:39.615
"This night can go
one of two ways.

287
00:25:39.695 --> 00:25:42.951
Either we can sit here drinking
alone with our thoughts,

288
00:25:43.031 --> 00:25:44.900
or we can make
something out of it."

289
00:25:46.401 --> 00:25:49.505
He smiled and ordered
a bottle of whiskey.

290
00:25:50.639 --> 00:25:51.907
This whiskey.

291
00:25:54.109 --> 00:25:56.632
And we talked about
everything under the sun.

292
00:25:56.712 --> 00:25:57.980
I mean everything.

293
00:25:59.748 --> 00:26:01.584
Our whole life story.

294
00:26:05.120 --> 00:26:08.811
I told him things I had
never told anyone before.

295
00:26:08.891 --> 00:26:12.315
I don't know, maybe it was the
storm or the booze, or...

296
00:26:12.395 --> 00:26:16.452
something, it just felt like
the last night of the world.

297
00:26:16.532 --> 00:26:19.422
We had so much to drink
that we go up to the roof

298
00:26:19.502 --> 00:26:23.359
with the idea that we'll make
it until the sun comes up,

299
00:26:23.439 --> 00:26:26.829
and we go out there in the
middle of this storm.

300
00:26:26.909 --> 00:26:28.698
I mean, the wind
is blowing hard.

301
00:26:28.778 --> 00:26:33.517
I remember thinking just how
powerful wind could actually be.

302
00:26:35.118 --> 00:26:39.489
Against the storm, my problems,
they seemed so trivial.

303
00:26:41.557 --> 00:26:44.947
We tried to get back inside,
but the door was locked,

304
00:26:45.027 --> 00:26:47.917
so we were stuck out
there all night.

305
00:26:47.997 --> 00:26:53.323
I woke up the next morning and
somehow I knew I'd be okay,

306
00:26:53.403 --> 00:26:55.405
that I'd live.

307
00:26:59.442 --> 00:27:02.045
Surviving that
storm, it saved me.

308
00:27:04.947 --> 00:27:06.469
If Gordon could still love life

309
00:27:06.549 --> 00:27:08.538
after everything
he'd been through,

310
00:27:08.618 --> 00:27:09.686
how could I not?

311
00:27:13.089 --> 00:27:14.878
He never told me that story.

312
00:27:14.958 --> 00:27:16.961
I've never told
anyone that story.

313
00:27:18.829 --> 00:27:20.751
We stayed in touch a few times,

314
00:27:20.831 --> 00:27:24.769
but really our friendship
was meant for one night.

315
00:27:27.471 --> 00:27:28.792
I'm a little bit jealous.

316
00:27:28.872 --> 00:27:31.128
It makes me feel like I
didn't know him anymore...

317
00:27:31.208 --> 00:27:34.679
not since our daughter...

318
00:27:36.647 --> 00:27:38.483
I guess maybe I didn't
for a long time.

319
00:27:39.883 --> 00:27:41.638
You can convince
yourself that you do,

320
00:27:41.718 --> 00:27:45.823
but then a complete stranger
shows up, tells you a story...

321
00:27:47.925 --> 00:27:50.681
then you realize, God, I
didn't even scratch the surface.

322
00:27:50.761 --> 00:27:52.149
Yeah, I see, I'm sorry.
I didn't...

323
00:27:52.229 --> 00:27:55.666
No, I'm... I'm really
glad that you did.

324
00:28:00.204 --> 00:28:02.507
Do you remember the last
conversation you had?

325
00:28:04.942 --> 00:28:06.778
Mm, I wish I didn't.

326
00:28:09.680 --> 00:28:11.616
I lost my brother.

327
00:28:12.683 --> 00:28:13.751
Long time ago.

328
00:28:14.785 --> 00:28:16.120
I'm sorry.

329
00:28:17.921 --> 00:28:21.044
I was there when he died.

330
00:28:21.124 --> 00:28:22.947
He said something to me,

331
00:28:23.027 --> 00:28:26.665
but I can't remember
what he said.

332
00:28:27.799 --> 00:28:30.955
I can remember every
detail of that night,

333
00:28:31.035 --> 00:28:33.691
except the last words
he said to me.

334
00:28:33.771 --> 00:28:37.609
It's funny how important
the details become.

335
00:28:39.878 --> 00:28:42.467
I haven't even been able to
go into Gordon's office yet.

336
00:28:42.547 --> 00:28:46.971
Everyone wants to get their hands
on his papers and his journals,

337
00:28:47.051 --> 00:28:50.809
and, frankly, it just seems easier
to just shut the world out.

338
00:28:50.889 --> 00:28:53.578
And all everyone wants to
talk about is his work,

339
00:28:53.658 --> 00:28:56.147
as if that's the most
important thing.

340
00:28:56.227 --> 00:29:02.520
It's not about his family or his
life or memories that he created,

341
00:29:02.600 --> 00:29:05.504
and it feels like they don't
really care about him.

342
00:29:07.872 --> 00:29:11.930
I mean, he was found dead in his
office with gunshots in the walls,

343
00:29:12.010 --> 00:29:14.213
and it's...

344
00:29:16.080 --> 00:29:17.616
Gunshots?

345
00:29:19.250 --> 00:29:21.739
Yeah, there's an investigation.

346
00:29:21.819 --> 00:29:25.610
I-I thought it was an aneurysm.
The papers said...

347
00:29:25.690 --> 00:29:30.516
His office was ransacked, and,
God knows, it's confusing.

348
00:29:30.596 --> 00:29:32.218
Do they know who
could've done that?

349
00:29:32.298 --> 00:29:35.621
He was working with a
lot of troubled people.

350
00:29:35.701 --> 00:29:37.556
I know that he was
having difficulties

351
00:29:37.636 --> 00:29:40.125
with some of the participants
of the research study.

352
00:29:40.205 --> 00:29:41.894
Have they found anything yet?

353
00:29:41.974 --> 00:29:43.896
I think we just had the funeral

354
00:29:43.976 --> 00:29:47.580
for the only person at Cortex
that could find anything.

355
00:29:49.248 --> 00:29:50.870
You think they're
hiding something?

356
00:29:50.950 --> 00:29:53.005
There's a part of me that
just thinks I just...

357
00:29:53.085 --> 00:29:57.590
I'm never gonna know the real truth,
and I didn't get to say goodbye.

358
00:30:01.894 --> 00:30:05.184
I just... sorry.

359
00:30:05.264 --> 00:30:08.687
I wish that I could've
been with him.

360
00:30:08.767 --> 00:30:10.737
Oh, God.

361
00:30:13.005 --> 00:30:13.993
Sorry, guh...

362
00:30:14.073 --> 00:30:15.227
It's okay.

363
00:30:15.307 --> 00:30:16.829
So stupid, I...

364
00:30:16.909 --> 00:30:21.066
I completely lost it this
morning because I was trying

365
00:30:21.146 --> 00:30:24.183
to get the coffee machine
to work, and I couldn't...

366
00:30:25.751 --> 00:30:28.941
and Gordon, he just, he
made the best coffee.

367
00:30:29.021 --> 00:30:33.846
You know, have coffee in the
morning, I cry like a baby.

368
00:30:33.926 --> 00:30:38.631
I cry at everything, I do.
Won't stop crying.

369
00:30:42.636 --> 00:30:44.171
Oh...

370
00:30:45.872 --> 00:30:51.865
James, would you mind awfully
telling me the story again?

371
00:30:51.945 --> 00:30:55.282
Oh, uh, sure.

372
00:30:58.218 --> 00:30:59.320
Cheers.

373
00:31:03.423 --> 00:31:06.193
It was a dark and
stormy night...

374
00:31:07.961 --> 00:31:09.349
Thank you, James.

375
00:31:09.429 --> 00:31:12.700
That was unexpected.

376
00:31:14.334 --> 00:31:16.723
I hope you'll visit again.

377
00:31:16.803 --> 00:31:18.592
I'm happy to.

378
00:31:18.672 --> 00:31:19.707
Bye.

379
00:32:17.732 --> 00:32:20.902
Pure, unfiltered truth.

380
00:32:56.471 --> 00:32:59.728
With the technology of
the Rememory machine,

381
00:32:59.808 --> 00:33:04.366
a pure memory is recorded
onto a memory glass

382
00:33:04.446 --> 00:33:07.202
objectively copied
from the brain

383
00:33:07.282 --> 00:33:08.837
and able to be displayed

384
00:33:08.917 --> 00:33:11.373
before the patient
and anyone else.

385
00:33:11.453 --> 00:33:13.875
By re-experiencing the memory,

386
00:33:13.955 --> 00:33:16.945
a catharsis is created
for the patient

387
00:33:17.025 --> 00:33:20.382
that would be impossible to
achieve by simply recalling,

388
00:33:20.462 --> 00:33:22.718
a sort of theater of memory,

389
00:33:22.798 --> 00:33:26.188
in which the patient
becomes audience

390
00:33:26.268 --> 00:33:28.504
to the truth of their lives.

391
00:33:36.945 --> 00:33:41.036
In the recording of a memory,

392
00:33:41.116 --> 00:33:44.773
the role of the psychologist
or therapist is crucial.

393
00:33:44.853 --> 00:33:48.810
The patient must be guided
to the memory to be recorded

394
00:33:48.890 --> 00:33:50.879
to activate that
part of the brain,

395
00:33:50.959 --> 00:33:53.515
even if it cannot be
remembered consciously.

396
00:33:53.595 --> 00:33:56.219
The machine has the potential
not only to access,

397
00:33:56.299 --> 00:34:00.356
but also to alter the
brain's synaptic pathways,

398
00:34:00.436 --> 00:34:02.091
so there is the
risk that memories

399
00:34:02.171 --> 00:34:06.295
can be corrupted,
altered, and jumbled.

400
00:34:06.375 --> 00:34:08.030
While it's
theoretically possible

401
00:34:08.110 --> 00:34:10.767
to constructively reset or
delete selected memories,

402
00:34:10.847 --> 00:34:13.850
at this time the
risks are unknown.

403
00:35:07.204 --> 00:35:08.573
Happy birth...

404
00:35:10.440 --> 00:35:12.510
Sam, honey, come on!

405
00:35:24.454 --> 00:35:26.157
Help me!

406
00:35:47.544 --> 00:35:49.399
Aw, it's good to
be home, brother.

407
00:35:49.479 --> 00:35:51.468
Aw, good to have
you back, brother.

408
00:35:51.548 --> 00:35:52.650
Yeah!

409
00:35:56.153 --> 00:35:57.875
I'm driving.

410
00:35:57.955 --> 00:36:00.377
No, no, no, no, this
is my sweet ride.

411
00:36:00.457 --> 00:36:02.412
Yeah, and I'm driving
your sweet ride.

412
00:36:02.492 --> 00:36:05.629
Okay. You're driving me...

413
00:36:27.051 --> 00:36:28.940
Other than the routine
security officers,

414
00:36:29.020 --> 00:36:30.275
janitors, and maintenance crew,

415
00:36:30.355 --> 00:36:33.077
only three people came
into Cortex after 11 p.m.

416
00:36:33.157 --> 00:36:34.979
using limited-access
swipe cards.

417
00:36:35.059 --> 00:36:36.547
However, none of them appear

418
00:36:36.627 --> 00:36:38.483
with the device on
any of our cameras.

419
00:36:38.563 --> 00:36:41.552
These two we've identified as
participants in Gordon's trial group,

420
00:36:41.632 --> 00:36:43.922
and that... that's Neil.

421
00:36:44.002 --> 00:36:47.191
Why would Gordon be meeting trial
group members here so late?

422
00:36:47.271 --> 00:36:49.060
- Rather out of the ordinary.
- That I don't know.

423
00:36:49.140 --> 00:36:51.029
But they arrived within
minutes of one another,

424
00:36:51.109 --> 00:36:52.931
so it could be a
scheduled meeting.

425
00:36:53.011 --> 00:36:56.167
Question all staff and crew
members working that night,

426
00:36:56.247 --> 00:36:58.303
and then visit all
trial group members.

427
00:36:58.383 --> 00:37:00.071
But be smart about it.

428
00:37:00.151 --> 00:37:02.073
We can't let it get out that
we don't have the machine.

429
00:37:02.153 --> 00:37:06.124
Uh, there's one other thing.

430
00:37:07.792 --> 00:37:09.347
This guy. He never came in,

431
00:37:09.427 --> 00:37:12.183
but he sat in the parking
lot for nearly 25 minutes.

432
00:37:12.263 --> 00:37:14.099
- Who is he?
- We don't know yet.

433
00:37:15.633 --> 00:37:16.735
Find out.

434
00:37:35.054 --> 00:37:37.543
I need to find out what
happened to you, Gordon.

435
00:37:37.623 --> 00:37:40.646
It's the least I can do.

436
00:37:40.726 --> 00:37:43.449
Maybe I can't see your memories,

437
00:37:43.529 --> 00:37:49.736
but I can see the memories of
those near you when you died.

438
00:38:11.123 --> 00:38:14.013
You like staying at
Grandma and Grandpa's?

439
00:38:14.093 --> 00:38:15.381
It's okay.

440
00:38:15.461 --> 00:38:19.051
They love having you.

441
00:38:19.131 --> 00:38:21.153
You're lucky that you
have grandparents

442
00:38:21.233 --> 00:38:23.156
and you're able to see them.

443
00:38:23.236 --> 00:38:25.725
There's lots of kids wish
they still had grandparents.

444
00:38:25.805 --> 00:38:27.808
But I want to stay with you.

445
00:38:29.409 --> 00:38:31.097
Mom.

446
00:38:31.177 --> 00:38:32.265
No.

447
00:38:32.345 --> 00:38:33.345
Mama!

448
00:38:50.230 --> 00:38:53.133
Okay, Gordon, let's
meet your group.

449
00:39:00.140 --> 00:39:02.328
Stay with me, come on!

450
00:39:02.408 --> 00:39:05.331
I want to help you get back
to the truth of that event.

451
00:39:05.411 --> 00:39:06.411
Go!

452
00:39:22.262 --> 00:39:26.233
Can you tell me a memory that
fills you with self-hate?

453
00:39:39.847 --> 00:39:43.852
When you keep this secret,
do you feel shame?

454
00:39:53.894 --> 00:39:56.250
Mom? Mommy!

455
00:39:56.330 --> 00:39:58.853
Good morning, birthday boy.
Please!

456
00:39:58.933 --> 00:40:01.870
Does thinking of this
always make you so angry?

457
00:40:08.609 --> 00:40:09.810
Do you miss your father?

458
00:40:17.885 --> 00:40:20.788
I can show you your past.

459
00:40:28.362 --> 00:40:29.362
I understand.

460
00:41:12.440 --> 00:41:16.231
It's gonna make his day to
see his Cousin Rafferty.

461
00:41:16.311 --> 00:41:17.732
I hope so.

462
00:41:17.812 --> 00:41:21.469
It's been forever
since I've seen him.

463
00:41:21.549 --> 00:41:24.786
Charles, look who's
here to see you!

464
00:41:26.021 --> 00:41:28.410
These plants, they
kept his brain alive.

465
00:41:28.490 --> 00:41:31.479
I swear he remembers all the days
of the week they need watering,

466
00:41:31.559 --> 00:41:33.615
but none of the
other nurses here.

467
00:41:33.695 --> 00:41:35.231
Isn't that right, Charles?

468
00:41:40.502 --> 00:41:43.839
Charles? Cousin Rafferty's
here to see you.

469
00:41:46.309 --> 00:41:47.544
I'll leave you to it.

470
00:41:48.744 --> 00:41:50.766
- Thank you.
- You're welcome.

471
00:41:50.846 --> 00:41:52.616
Hugh, is that you?

472
00:41:53.716 --> 00:41:56.239
No... my name is Rafferty.

473
00:41:56.319 --> 00:41:59.642
Charles, do you
remember Gordon Dunn?

474
00:41:59.722 --> 00:42:01.410
The doctor?

475
00:42:01.490 --> 00:42:02.959
Do I know you?

476
00:42:09.932 --> 00:42:11.587
You brought it.

477
00:42:11.667 --> 00:42:13.503
Do you want to watch?

478
00:42:14.837 --> 00:42:17.474
Yes, please.

479
00:42:21.344 --> 00:42:22.344
Come on.

480
00:43:08.091 --> 00:43:11.629
Okay, Buster. All right.

481
00:43:27.177 --> 00:43:32.450
My life. That's my life.

482
00:43:35.685 --> 00:43:39.423
Cindy was... was my wife.

483
00:43:40.690 --> 00:43:44.481
I was always true to her.

484
00:43:44.561 --> 00:43:48.018
Charles, who are
the other people

485
00:43:48.098 --> 00:43:49.753
in the group working
with Gordon?

486
00:43:49.833 --> 00:43:51.569
I need to find them.

487
00:43:53.103 --> 00:43:57.028
I remember one, a
woman, Allison.

488
00:43:57.108 --> 00:44:00.365
She came here to see
me a few times.

489
00:44:00.445 --> 00:44:02.147
Do you remember her last name?

490
00:44:04.982 --> 00:44:06.137
She never told me.

491
00:44:06.217 --> 00:44:09.774
Just one more question,

492
00:44:09.854 --> 00:44:11.523
if you can remember.

493
00:44:13.458 --> 00:44:15.012
When you were recording
your memories,

494
00:44:15.092 --> 00:44:19.698
what did Gordon tell you about
going back to a specific moment?

495
00:44:23.801 --> 00:44:27.558
It was all about focus...

496
00:44:27.638 --> 00:44:28.673
Focus?

497
00:44:36.614 --> 00:44:41.506
I thought I told you, you
have to let the water drain.

498
00:44:41.586 --> 00:44:44.556
- Um, okay.
- I'll look at it myself.

499
00:44:49.126 --> 00:44:50.695
Thank you, Charles.

500
00:45:04.109 --> 00:45:05.645
Let it drain.

501
00:45:23.061 --> 00:45:25.083
Yeah, sweets.

502
00:45:25.163 --> 00:45:26.718
Good afternoon, Mrs. Dunn.

503
00:45:26.798 --> 00:45:27.719
Good afternoon.

504
00:45:27.799 --> 00:45:31.123
So, we got the results of
the toxicology report.

505
00:45:31.203 --> 00:45:34.126
- And?
- Well, there were a few spikes,

506
00:45:34.206 --> 00:45:37.796
but nothing that indicate
anything unusual.

507
00:45:37.876 --> 00:45:39.698
Well, what about the gunshots?

508
00:45:39.778 --> 00:45:41.667
It's still an ongoing
investigation.

509
00:45:41.747 --> 00:45:43.202
Now, we're still
running ballistics,

510
00:45:43.282 --> 00:45:45.804
and Cortex is cooperating
with background checks

511
00:45:45.884 --> 00:45:47.139
as well as security footage.

512
00:45:47.219 --> 00:45:49.141
Eh, Lawton came 'round

513
00:45:49.221 --> 00:45:51.076
this morning to reassure me.

514
00:45:51.156 --> 00:45:54.146
I think he just wanted to
get into Gordon's office.

515
00:45:54.226 --> 00:45:57.483
Was everything all right
between you and Gordon?

516
00:45:57.563 --> 00:45:59.799
Mm. Goodbye, Detective.

517
00:46:05.237 --> 00:46:06.672
I'll be in touch.

518
00:46:18.351 --> 00:46:21.641
- Allison?
- She's not here.

519
00:46:21.721 --> 00:46:22.576
Can I help you?

520
00:46:22.656 --> 00:46:25.778
Yes, my name is Basil.
I work for Cortex.

521
00:46:25.858 --> 00:46:27.780
She's part of an
experiment group for us,

522
00:46:27.860 --> 00:46:29.949
and it's my job to check
in on the participants

523
00:46:30.029 --> 00:46:32.652
from time to time and make
sure they're doing well.

524
00:46:32.732 --> 00:46:34.787
- You work for Cortex?
- Yes, ma'am.

525
00:46:34.867 --> 00:46:36.122
And what is your name?

526
00:46:36.202 --> 00:46:38.625
Basil. Basil Pine.

527
00:46:38.705 --> 00:46:41.528
Well, Basil, if that's the case,

528
00:46:41.608 --> 00:46:44.211
you are truly shit at your job.

529
00:46:45.945 --> 00:46:46.966
I'm sorry?

530
00:46:47.046 --> 00:46:48.668
Allison is dead.

531
00:46:48.748 --> 00:46:51.070
She killed herself
three weeks ago.

532
00:46:51.150 --> 00:46:53.186
You didn't know.

533
00:46:55.788 --> 00:46:58.244
- I'm sorry.
- Sorry doesn't bring back my sister.

534
00:46:58.324 --> 00:47:01.214
She was too fragile to be part
of your idiotic experiment.

535
00:47:01.294 --> 00:47:04.217
Anyone with half an eye
could've seen that.

536
00:47:04.297 --> 00:47:05.766
I have her memories.

537
00:47:12.038 --> 00:47:15.776
I know about the difficulties
in your past, Allison.

538
00:47:29.757 --> 00:47:31.826
I know the medications
aren't working.

539
00:47:33.994 --> 00:47:35.082
I can help.

540
00:47:35.162 --> 00:47:37.632
Happy birthday, Alli-boo!

541
00:47:41.101 --> 00:47:42.923
This may take a while.

542
00:47:43.003 --> 00:47:44.258
It's a process.

543
00:47:44.338 --> 00:47:46.908
It may get worse
before it gets better.

544
00:47:52.713 --> 00:47:55.903
That birthday, we surprised her.

545
00:47:55.983 --> 00:47:59.273
That was the last time
I saw her truly happy.

546
00:47:59.353 --> 00:48:01.823
Do you think she
blamed the machine?

547
00:48:03.924 --> 00:48:08.663
I think it suffocated
her in her own past.

548
00:48:12.399 --> 00:48:15.022
What's the point
of this, anyway?

549
00:48:15.102 --> 00:48:16.890
Why does the machine exist?

550
00:48:16.970 --> 00:48:18.292
I'm not sure, exactly.

551
00:48:18.372 --> 00:48:23.030
But Gordon Dunn believed
that we don't know

552
00:48:23.110 --> 00:48:26.101
the true value of a moment
until it becomes a memory.

553
00:48:26.181 --> 00:48:30.639
Maybe he was trying to get people
to see the value of a moment

554
00:48:30.719 --> 00:48:33.975
before it changes or
gets lost forever.

555
00:48:34.055 --> 00:48:37.145
Well, that's very nice
and flowery, but...

556
00:48:37.225 --> 00:48:40.181
Did anyone from
Cortex contact you?

557
00:48:40.261 --> 00:48:42.017
Gordon, or someone
from his group?

558
00:48:42.097 --> 00:48:46.655
One phone call from
some guy named Robert.

559
00:48:46.735 --> 00:48:48.857
- Lawton?
- That's right.

560
00:48:48.937 --> 00:48:52.727
He offered me a check as long
as I didn't talk to anyone.

561
00:48:52.807 --> 00:48:54.696
- Did you take it?
- Of course I took it.

562
00:48:54.776 --> 00:48:57.298
Who couldn't use $50,000?

563
00:48:57.378 --> 00:49:00.802
Anyway, you can't fight these corporations.
You never win.

564
00:49:00.882 --> 00:49:01.882
No.

565
00:49:06.187 --> 00:49:08.357
I'm very sorry
about your sister.

566
00:49:11.926 --> 00:49:15.750
You know, there was
another guy came by,

567
00:49:15.830 --> 00:49:18.987
a young guy, kind of strange.

568
00:49:19.067 --> 00:49:22.424
He said he was a researcher
from the doctor... Gordon.

569
00:49:22.504 --> 00:49:24.693
What did he want?

570
00:49:24.773 --> 00:49:26.861
To know if Allison
really killed herself.

571
00:49:26.941 --> 00:49:28.096
That was all.

572
00:49:28.176 --> 00:49:29.964
He seemed shocked
when I said yes.

573
00:49:30.044 --> 00:49:31.233
You remember his name?

574
00:49:31.313 --> 00:49:34.370
Neil Frankel.

575
00:49:34.450 --> 00:49:36.439
- The valentine.
- What's that?

576
00:49:36.519 --> 00:49:39.975
Nothing, just trying
to remember the name.

577
00:49:40.055 --> 00:49:41.277
Mm.

578
00:49:41.357 --> 00:49:43.879
Well, that's what he said.

579
00:49:43.959 --> 00:49:47.063
But then again, you said
your name was Basil Pine.

580
00:50:31.106 --> 00:50:35.030
♪ Fill yourself with quarters ♪

581
00:50:35.110 --> 00:50:38.969
♪ Showered and blue-blazered ♪

582
00:50:39.049 --> 00:50:42.339
♪ Fill yourself with quarters ♪

583
00:50:42.419 --> 00:50:44.441
Somebody help me!

584
00:50:44.521 --> 00:50:45.856
Please!

585
00:51:52.290 --> 00:51:53.578
Who are you working for?

586
00:51:53.658 --> 00:51:57.081
An interested party.
Who are you?

587
00:51:57.161 --> 00:52:00.051
I worked with Gordon. I
was a research assistant.

588
00:52:00.131 --> 00:52:01.131
Neil Frankel?

589
00:52:02.934 --> 00:52:04.155
How did you find me?

590
00:52:04.235 --> 00:52:06.324
I remembered your face
from the product launch.

591
00:52:06.404 --> 00:52:07.859
I tracked you down.

592
00:52:07.939 --> 00:52:09.994
- Pretty good memory.
- It's photographic.

593
00:52:10.074 --> 00:52:12.230
That's the reason why Gordon wanted
me a part of the test group.

594
00:52:12.310 --> 00:52:14.899
- He found it curious.
- What do you want from me?

595
00:52:14.979 --> 00:52:16.081
The machine.

596
00:52:17.348 --> 00:52:18.936
Is it missing?

597
00:52:19.016 --> 00:52:21.005
- We need the machine!
- Who's we?

598
00:52:21.085 --> 00:52:24.141
- Cortex!
- I don't... I don't have it.

599
00:52:24.221 --> 00:52:26.524
But I can help you find
it if you help me.

600
00:52:27.959 --> 00:52:30.147
- How?
- Tell me what led to Allison's suicide.

601
00:52:30.227 --> 00:52:32.350
You're not supposed
to know about that.

602
00:52:32.430 --> 00:52:33.551
There are people
looking for you...

603
00:52:33.631 --> 00:52:35.052
A man has been murdered.

604
00:52:35.132 --> 00:52:36.621
We got bigger things
to worry about.

605
00:52:36.701 --> 00:52:38.356
- It was an aneurysm.
- That's what they say,

606
00:52:38.436 --> 00:52:41.259
but if you trusted Lawton we
wouldn't be meeting like this.

607
00:52:41.339 --> 00:52:43.108
Unless this is a set up.

608
00:52:44.442 --> 00:52:45.963
Why did you go see Allison?

609
00:52:46.043 --> 00:52:47.431
I wanted to see if it was true.

610
00:52:47.511 --> 00:52:50.369
About her suicide?
But she'd been dead

611
00:52:50.449 --> 00:52:51.670
for weeks when you got there.

612
00:52:51.750 --> 00:52:54.239
I didn't know that. Not
even Gordon knew...

613
00:52:54.319 --> 00:52:55.507
How could you not know?

614
00:52:55.587 --> 00:52:57.409
Her test phases were over, it...

615
00:52:57.489 --> 00:52:59.311
Lawton didn't want us to know.

616
00:52:59.391 --> 00:53:00.612
He was hiding it.

617
00:53:00.692 --> 00:53:02.948
Yes, could you imagine the
headlines if it got out

618
00:53:03.028 --> 00:53:04.683
that a patient had killed
herself during trials?

619
00:53:04.763 --> 00:53:08.487
That would've been the story. It
would've ended the whole thing.

620
00:53:08.567 --> 00:53:10.022
By the time Gordon found out,

621
00:53:10.102 --> 00:53:11.490
he had no choice but to
continue with the launch.

622
00:53:11.570 --> 00:53:13.525
But he didn't know everything.

623
00:53:13.605 --> 00:53:15.594
The research, it's flawed.

624
00:53:15.674 --> 00:53:18.096
- Lawton manipulated it.
- Why would they do that?

625
00:53:18.176 --> 00:53:20.933
Because a not so small
percentage of people

626
00:53:21.013 --> 00:53:24.069
were prone to adverse
effects of the machine.

627
00:53:24.149 --> 00:53:25.070
Adverse how?

628
00:53:25.150 --> 00:53:27.940
Explicit and emotional
memories getting confused

629
00:53:28.020 --> 00:53:29.341
and playing out in reality,

630
00:53:29.421 --> 00:53:32.725
over-firing of synapses,
hallucinations, time collapse.

631
00:53:34.326 --> 00:53:36.581
Just like Allison.

632
00:53:36.661 --> 00:53:38.517
If Gordon had known,
he would've fixed it,

633
00:53:38.597 --> 00:53:43.221
I don't have a doubt, but he
would've needed time, months,

634
00:53:43.301 --> 00:53:45.490
and that's something
that Lawton didn't have.

635
00:53:45.570 --> 00:53:47.693
He made certain promises.

636
00:53:47.773 --> 00:53:50.595
There are issues
to be figured out,

637
00:53:50.675 --> 00:53:53.598
but the machine is incredible.

638
00:53:53.678 --> 00:53:55.982
What it can do for people, it...

639
00:53:57.316 --> 00:54:00.373
Gordon deserves to be
remembered as a genius.

640
00:54:00.453 --> 00:54:03.009
I think we're done here.

641
00:54:03.089 --> 00:54:05.211
If you find the machine,
you know where I work.

642
00:54:05.291 --> 00:54:09.029
I'm looking for a young woman
who was part of the test group.

643
00:54:10.430 --> 00:54:13.166
Well, there were two, and
now one of them's dead.

644
00:54:52.472 --> 00:54:56.162
Mom? Mommy?

645
00:54:56.242 --> 00:54:57.277
Please.

646
00:55:03.382 --> 00:55:04.818
I love you.

647
00:55:10.524 --> 00:55:12.260
Gordon.

648
00:55:30.344 --> 00:55:34.449
We've tracked down the group,
but none have the machine.

649
00:55:36.717 --> 00:55:39.153
I was saving this...

650
00:55:41.622 --> 00:55:42.843
for Gordon's return.

651
00:55:42.923 --> 00:55:45.379
I'm sure he would've wanted you

652
00:55:45.459 --> 00:55:47.295
to enjoy it nonetheless.

653
00:55:50.430 --> 00:55:51.819
Do you have any idea

654
00:55:51.899 --> 00:55:57.405
the amount of pressure
crushing down on me?

655
00:56:00.841 --> 00:56:02.176
We'll find it.

656
00:56:03.710 --> 00:56:05.146
Yes, you will.

657
00:56:15.557 --> 00:56:17.426
Good morning, birthday boy.

658
00:56:21.229 --> 00:56:22.150
Please.

659
00:56:22.230 --> 00:56:23.585
Does thinking of this

660
00:56:23.665 --> 00:56:25.720
always make you so angry?

661
00:56:25.800 --> 00:56:28.470
Wendy, this will help.

662
00:56:32.607 --> 00:56:34.743
So, when you think of it,
do you still feel fear?

663
00:56:36.211 --> 00:56:39.601
- Mom?
- Does your heart rate increase?

664
00:56:39.681 --> 00:56:41.183
Mommy!

665
00:56:42.684 --> 00:56:43.886
I understand.

666
00:57:18.454 --> 00:57:20.490
I'll be here if you
have any questions.

667
00:57:29.832 --> 00:57:31.801
It's beautiful, isn't it?

668
00:57:32.668 --> 00:57:33.803
Exquisite.

669
00:57:34.703 --> 00:57:36.425
Reminds me of a memory.

670
00:57:36.505 --> 00:57:38.761
Yeah? Of what?

671
00:57:38.841 --> 00:57:40.696
My father.

672
00:57:40.776 --> 00:57:43.365
He was a real outdoors man.

673
00:57:43.445 --> 00:57:46.468
Hunting, fishing,
hiking, all of it.

674
00:57:46.548 --> 00:57:48.971
One day, he took my brother
and I out for a hunt.

675
00:57:49.051 --> 00:57:52.841
It was rare that he
took both of us.

676
00:57:52.921 --> 00:57:57.360
We dug a hole in the
mud and waited.

677
00:57:58.427 --> 00:58:00.530
I just wanted to make him proud.

678
00:58:02.431 --> 00:58:06.522
After about an hour,
a rabbit popped up.

679
00:58:06.602 --> 00:58:08.257
I grabbed my gun,

680
00:58:08.337 --> 00:58:11.594
and he went from a quick
hop to a straight drop.

681
00:58:11.674 --> 00:58:15.297
Blood everywhere.

682
00:58:15.377 --> 00:58:18.767
Yeah, kind of wish I
could forget that one.

683
00:58:18.847 --> 00:58:21.637
You have something you
wish you could forget?

684
00:58:21.717 --> 00:58:25.255
Or someone? Gordon Dunn, maybe?

685
00:58:28.591 --> 00:58:29.613
Who are you?

686
00:58:29.693 --> 00:58:31.328
I'm working for his widow.

687
00:58:32.028 --> 00:58:34.265
I know about you and him.

688
00:58:37.834 --> 00:58:40.257
- You think he was killed?
- I do.

689
00:58:40.337 --> 00:58:43.593
And I know you saw
him that night.

690
00:58:43.673 --> 00:58:45.395
I know you stole the machine.

691
00:58:45.475 --> 00:58:48.531
I loved him, and he loved me.

692
00:58:48.611 --> 00:58:50.481
Was it over?

693
00:58:52.615 --> 00:58:53.937
It's not my choice.

694
00:58:54.017 --> 00:58:55.939
Is that why you
took the machine?

695
00:58:56.019 --> 00:58:57.540
To get back at him?

696
00:58:57.620 --> 00:59:01.811
While I was working with
Gordon one session,

697
00:59:01.891 --> 00:59:04.981
he... he saw something.

698
00:59:05.061 --> 00:59:08.785
It was nothing incriminating,
it was just this memory

699
00:59:08.865 --> 00:59:12.069
from a few years ago
with the man I loved.

700
00:59:13.036 --> 00:59:13.990
A private moment.

701
00:59:14.070 --> 00:59:16.059
He had no right to even see it.

702
00:59:16.139 --> 00:59:17.908
So I wanted it back.

703
00:59:20.377 --> 00:59:23.800
Imagine, to have your memories
watched by other people,

704
00:59:23.880 --> 00:59:25.635
in front of an audience?

705
00:59:25.715 --> 00:59:28.319
It was like my life
wasn't my own anymore.

706
00:59:30.653 --> 00:59:33.390
Every memory became
clouded with Gordon.

707
00:59:34.392 --> 00:59:36.561
But you didn't get them back.

708
00:59:38.462 --> 00:59:39.583
I tried.

709
00:59:39.663 --> 00:59:41.686
I took the machine from Cortex

710
00:59:41.766 --> 00:59:44.969
and I went back to
Gordon's house.

711
00:59:56.514 --> 00:59:58.469
I just wanted to get
my memories back.

712
00:59:58.549 --> 01:00:00.952
I didn't care about the rest.

713
01:00:13.164 --> 01:00:14.999
- But then...
- Gordon?

714
01:00:18.836 --> 01:00:19.971
Gordon?

715
01:00:28.446 --> 01:00:29.567
Gordon?

716
01:00:29.647 --> 01:00:31.635
I even tried to go back,

717
01:00:31.715 --> 01:00:33.037
but everything was gone.

718
01:00:33.117 --> 01:00:35.473
I can't move on from this

719
01:00:35.553 --> 01:00:37.989
until I have my memories back.

720
01:00:41.026 --> 01:00:43.129
If you help me find
who killed Gordon...

721
01:00:44.629 --> 01:00:46.799
I'll get them back for you.

722
01:00:50.602 --> 01:00:53.058
If you haven't talked to
Todd yet, you should.

723
01:00:53.138 --> 01:00:54.793
He was furious.

724
01:00:54.873 --> 01:00:57.076
He was there that night?

725
01:00:59.044 --> 01:01:01.032
Gordon was...

726
01:01:01.112 --> 01:01:03.769
he was a brilliant man,

727
01:01:03.849 --> 01:01:05.718
and a truly good man.

728
01:01:07.586 --> 01:01:10.022
I still haven't decided
if his creation was.

729
01:01:11.256 --> 01:01:13.692
Yeah, neither have I.

730
01:01:17.496 --> 01:01:19.532
Why are you doing this?

731
01:01:21.566 --> 01:01:22.835
Resolution.

732
01:01:36.915 --> 01:01:40.619
It has one button. Off and on.

733
01:01:47.693 --> 01:01:49.549
Med school. Oxford.

734
01:01:49.629 --> 01:01:51.651
Specifically, the
woods behind the lab.

735
01:01:51.731 --> 01:01:55.121
Gordon was testing on animals
and I was freeing them,

736
01:01:55.201 --> 01:01:57.023
and he got worried that someone

737
01:01:57.103 --> 01:01:59.125
was busting out the primates,

738
01:01:59.205 --> 01:02:00.793
so he came after me.

739
01:02:00.873 --> 01:02:05.465
He found me in the woods
feeding sandwiches to Pickles.

740
01:02:05.545 --> 01:02:06.545
Pickles?

741
01:02:08.981 --> 01:02:10.036
Pickles the chimp.

742
01:02:10.116 --> 01:02:11.218
Okay.

743
01:02:15.288 --> 01:02:16.809
It was a whole other time.

744
01:02:16.889 --> 01:02:18.744
Funny how life goes, huh?

745
01:02:18.824 --> 01:02:21.848
Years pass, and slowly you
find yourself defined

746
01:02:21.928 --> 01:02:23.716
not so much by the
things that you do,

747
01:02:23.796 --> 01:02:25.151
but the things
that you don't do.

748
01:02:25.231 --> 01:02:27.587
I was gonna learn
French, write a book,

749
01:02:27.667 --> 01:02:31.491
I was gonna have a happy,
huge, sprawling family,

750
01:02:31.571 --> 01:02:33.626
and one by one, I
didn't do those things,

751
01:02:33.706 --> 01:02:35.528
or those things didn't happen.

752
01:02:35.608 --> 01:02:37.711
And I am what is left.

753
01:02:39.712 --> 01:02:44.150
I guess, in part, we're all
remains of unfulfilled dreams.

754
01:02:46.852 --> 01:02:52.559
After Jane died, Gordon wanted
to have another, and I couldn't.

755
01:02:54.227 --> 01:02:56.617
So he threw himself
into his work.

756
01:02:56.697 --> 01:03:00.120
I think he really believed

757
01:03:00.200 --> 01:03:02.789
that the machine could
change the past,

758
01:03:02.869 --> 01:03:05.726
and when he realized
that he... it didn't...

759
01:03:05.806 --> 01:03:11.279
He found himself another woman
who didn't remind him of that.

760
01:03:14.314 --> 01:03:16.003
And I still loved him.

761
01:03:16.083 --> 01:03:18.105
I will always love him.

762
01:03:18.185 --> 01:03:21.608
He was brilliant.

763
01:03:21.688 --> 01:03:23.858
And the last thing
that I said to him...

764
01:03:24.891 --> 01:03:26.193
was "You're an asshole."

765
01:03:29.997 --> 01:03:33.768
And I have to live with that
memory and that reality and...

766
01:03:35.235 --> 01:03:39.026
God, it just feels like
there's no ending.

767
01:03:39.106 --> 01:03:41.662
I think there will
be an end to it.

768
01:03:41.742 --> 01:03:44.031
I believe that.

769
01:03:44.111 --> 01:03:48.802
And if we are, as Gordon said,
just the sum of our memories,

770
01:03:48.882 --> 01:03:52.753
I'm really glad to have
made this one with you.

771
01:05:27.316 --> 01:05:32.755
I can't think of a time in my
life when I've been happier.

772
01:05:55.210 --> 01:05:56.779
One left.

773
01:06:03.819 --> 01:06:07.323
TODD

774
01:06:33.983 --> 01:06:35.819
No. No!

775
01:06:57.540 --> 01:06:59.076
Excuse me. Hello.

776
01:07:02.412 --> 01:07:04.134
- Pickup?
- What?

777
01:07:04.214 --> 01:07:06.803
Do you have a pickup?

778
01:07:06.883 --> 01:07:09.987
- Uh, no.
- So, how can I help you?

779
01:07:13.256 --> 01:07:15.526
I wanted to ask you a few
questions about Gordon Dunn.

780
01:07:17.327 --> 01:07:18.983
Get out of here.

781
01:07:19.063 --> 01:07:21.318
- Okay. Just a few questions...
- Get out of here.

782
01:07:21.398 --> 01:07:23.287
- Just a few quest...
- Get out!

783
01:07:23.367 --> 01:07:24.903
Hey. Ahem.

784
01:07:50.394 --> 01:07:52.897
Sir. Ahem.

785
01:07:54.531 --> 01:07:56.587
How did you know the
combination to the safe?

786
01:07:56.667 --> 01:07:59.871
I-I've seen your memories, Todd.

787
01:08:00.604 --> 01:08:03.141
What do you want from me?

788
01:08:05.576 --> 01:08:06.864
Did you kill Gordon Dunn?

789
01:08:06.944 --> 01:08:09.066
Put it down. Put it down!

790
01:08:09.146 --> 01:08:11.535
I know he was killed, I
know you were fighting,

791
01:08:11.615 --> 01:08:13.437
and I know you were
at Cortex that night.

792
01:08:13.517 --> 01:08:15.272
I didn't kill him. Put it down!

793
01:08:15.352 --> 01:08:16.974
Just... why were you fighting?

794
01:08:17.054 --> 01:08:21.912
Okay. I'll tell you.
I'll tell you.

795
01:08:21.992 --> 01:08:23.862
I'll tell you. Just put it down.

796
01:08:27.365 --> 01:08:29.454
I wanted to kill him.

797
01:08:29.534 --> 01:08:32.170
I really did.

798
01:08:33.672 --> 01:08:36.242
He didn't tell us what
the machine would do.

799
01:08:37.442 --> 01:08:39.397
Not at all.

800
01:08:39.477 --> 01:08:42.033
He didn't tell us it would...

801
01:08:42.113 --> 01:08:45.570
bring back things
that we had buried.

802
01:08:45.650 --> 01:08:50.008
You know, the mind forgets
things for a reason.

803
01:08:50.088 --> 01:08:52.444
You didn't listen!
You never listened!

804
01:08:52.524 --> 01:08:54.059
And now it's too late.

805
01:08:55.093 --> 01:08:55.981
Take it easy.

806
01:08:56.061 --> 01:08:57.883
Calm down, please. Please.

807
01:08:57.963 --> 01:08:59.517
Take it easy. Calm down.

808
01:08:59.597 --> 01:09:01.019
No, you don't want to...

809
01:09:01.099 --> 01:09:02.988
Please, don't let this escalate.

810
01:09:03.068 --> 01:09:05.137
No, no. No!

811
01:09:09.507 --> 01:09:10.910
When I woke up...

812
01:09:11.977 --> 01:09:13.913
in the hospital, I had...

813
01:09:14.746 --> 01:09:16.949
I'd forgotten everything.

814
01:09:18.183 --> 01:09:20.171
And then the machine
brought it all back,

815
01:09:20.251 --> 01:09:23.642
and it wouldn't stop
playing in my mind.

816
01:09:23.722 --> 01:09:27.660
Her getting shot over...

817
01:09:29.227 --> 01:09:30.296
and over.

818
01:09:31.563 --> 01:09:33.586
I saw it everywhere.

819
01:09:33.666 --> 01:09:35.969
I couldn't turn it off.

820
01:09:37.169 --> 01:09:40.040
It was my fault.

821
01:09:43.409 --> 01:09:45.298
And then I found out...

822
01:09:45.378 --> 01:09:48.334
that Gordon knew about the
shooting the whole time.

823
01:09:48.414 --> 01:09:50.970
He chose you because he knew
what you'd been through.

824
01:09:51.050 --> 01:09:54.473
He ruined my life,
you understand?

825
01:09:54.553 --> 01:09:57.643
I asked him to
delete that memory,

826
01:09:57.723 --> 01:10:01.547
and he wouldn't do it!

827
01:10:01.627 --> 01:10:03.082
He brought back a memory

828
01:10:03.162 --> 01:10:06.285
that my brain had
erased for a reason.

829
01:10:06.365 --> 01:10:11.738
So, yeah, I was there in that
office that night to kill him.

830
01:10:14.607 --> 01:10:16.495
And this time...

831
01:10:16.575 --> 01:10:19.212
I had my finger on the trigger.

832
01:10:21.447 --> 01:10:23.183
And I couldn't do it.

833
01:10:26.719 --> 01:10:31.124
I knew I couldn't live with it.

834
01:10:42.469 --> 01:10:43.469
Prove it.

835
01:11:08.361 --> 01:11:10.283
Tell my life...

836
01:11:10.363 --> 01:11:12.219
Gordon...

837
01:11:12.299 --> 01:11:14.187
Gordon...

838
01:11:14.267 --> 01:11:15.436
Gordon!

839
01:11:20.774 --> 01:11:22.729
Uh, Todd, what are
you doing here?

840
01:11:22.809 --> 01:11:24.698
You fucked up my head!

841
01:11:24.778 --> 01:11:27.481
You came to me injured and hurt.

842
01:11:28.582 --> 01:11:33.707
No. No, you, you came to me.

843
01:11:33.787 --> 01:11:38.092
My life? My life
was better before.

844
01:11:40.660 --> 01:11:41.715
Jesus, Todd.

845
01:11:41.795 --> 01:11:43.483
You ruined my life,

846
01:11:43.563 --> 01:11:46.154
and now you're gonna
make a bunch of money?

847
01:11:46.234 --> 01:11:49.404
- That's not why.
- You don't give a shit about anyone!

848
01:11:53.474 --> 01:11:54.676
Listen to me.

849
01:11:56.878 --> 01:11:58.633
You know, when I fall,

850
01:11:58.713 --> 01:12:00.234
my eyes roll back,

851
01:12:00.314 --> 01:12:02.784
blood splattered... blood
splattered everywhere...

852
01:12:04.418 --> 01:12:08.223
that's not a memory you want.

853
01:12:10.558 --> 01:12:14.763
It's better than what
you've done to me.

854
01:12:18.699 --> 01:12:20.302
Don't do it, Todd.

855
01:13:00.542 --> 01:13:02.545
She lied to me.

856
01:13:06.982 --> 01:13:09.185
Why would Wendy
want to set you up?

857
01:13:10.586 --> 01:13:14.524
She blamed me for Gordon
breaking it off with her.

858
01:13:15.591 --> 01:13:17.679
Why?

859
01:13:17.759 --> 01:13:19.848
Because when I found
out about them,

860
01:13:19.928 --> 01:13:21.517
I threatened Gordon with it.

861
01:13:21.597 --> 01:13:24.901
Told them I'd tell his
wife if he didn't fix me.

862
01:13:25.968 --> 01:13:27.756
He talked me back,

863
01:13:27.836 --> 01:13:29.791
but right after he
broke up with her.

864
01:13:29.871 --> 01:13:31.560
I need to see what she saw.

865
01:13:31.640 --> 01:13:33.295
That's not gonna happen.

866
01:13:33.375 --> 01:13:35.197
She's not going back in there.

867
01:13:35.277 --> 01:13:36.913
I'll get her on the machine.

868
01:13:38.614 --> 01:13:40.249
I wouldn't be so sure.

869
01:13:41.850 --> 01:13:44.220
I have what she wants.

870
01:13:54.296 --> 01:13:57.419
Ah, a brand-new tent, going
camping up at Frost Lake!

871
01:13:57.499 --> 01:13:59.956
Whoo! It's another
five miles to hike,

872
01:14:00.036 --> 01:14:01.591
but it gets us past
all the tourists.

873
01:14:01.671 --> 01:14:04.194
We'll have it to ourselves.
What do you say, brother?

874
01:14:04.274 --> 01:14:05.528
You're not here, Dash.

875
01:14:05.608 --> 01:14:07.730
What are you talking about?
Of course I'm here.

876
01:14:07.810 --> 01:14:10.400
- We're going camping, man.
- You're not here.

877
01:14:10.480 --> 01:14:12.802
You're dead.

878
01:14:12.882 --> 01:14:14.804
Dead, what a stupid
thing to say.

879
01:14:14.884 --> 01:14:16.954
Of course I'm here.
I'm not dead.

880
01:14:21.291 --> 01:14:24.681
What... Where you going, man?

881
01:14:24.761 --> 01:14:28.651
What is this? What the...
What the hell is going on?

882
01:14:28.731 --> 01:14:30.520
I'm right here!

883
01:14:30.600 --> 01:14:32.422
You're dead, Dash,
and it's my fault.

884
01:14:32.502 --> 01:14:34.490
That doesn't sound like a
brotherly thing to do.

885
01:14:34.570 --> 01:14:35.892
I couldn't let Dad blame
me for your death.

886
01:14:35.972 --> 01:14:37.860
It would only prove him right.

887
01:14:37.940 --> 01:14:40.697
I had to ruin your name
not to ruin my life!

888
01:14:40.777 --> 01:14:43.013
You did more than ruin my name.

889
01:14:55.458 --> 01:14:58.648
We found him. He came
to see the old man.

890
01:14:58.728 --> 01:14:59.949
He matches the description,

891
01:15:00.029 --> 01:15:02.752
and the nurses said he was
carrying a briefcase.

892
01:15:02.832 --> 01:15:04.921
Same one Gordon used to bring.

893
01:15:05.001 --> 01:15:06.924
Let me take care of it.

894
01:15:07.004 --> 01:15:08.473
Understood.

895
01:15:15.346 --> 01:15:16.934
- James?
- Hi, can I come in?

896
01:15:17.014 --> 01:15:18.483
I need to talk to you.

897
01:15:19.583 --> 01:15:20.583
Are you okay?

898
01:15:21.685 --> 01:15:23.621
James, what's the matter?

899
01:15:25.389 --> 01:15:26.944
I need to tell you something.

900
01:15:27.024 --> 01:15:28.946
I haven't been completely
honest with you.

901
01:15:29.026 --> 01:15:31.315
- Why do you have that?
- I took it.

902
01:15:31.395 --> 01:15:34.852
You stole it? That's
Gordon's life's work.

903
01:15:34.932 --> 01:15:37.788
I'm trying to figure out what
happened to him. I'm so close!

904
01:15:37.868 --> 01:15:39.823
This machine, it's...

905
01:15:39.903 --> 01:15:41.506
Who is that?

906
01:15:44.375 --> 01:15:47.364
- Oh, it's Lawton.
- Oh, shit.

907
01:15:47.444 --> 01:15:48.966
You can't let him
know that I'm here.

908
01:15:49.046 --> 01:15:50.834
You stole the machine.
Why wouldn't I?

909
01:15:50.914 --> 01:15:52.569
Because I think I know
who killed Gordon,

910
01:15:52.649 --> 01:15:55.339
and Lawton has been lying.
He lied to you, Carolyn...

911
01:15:55.419 --> 01:15:57.322
and he lied to Gordon.

912
01:15:58.589 --> 01:16:00.325
Please, Carolyn, you
have to believe me.

913
01:16:09.066 --> 01:16:12.670
- Robert.
- I need to talk to you, Carolyn.

914
01:16:14.839 --> 01:16:16.075
Come in.

915
01:16:26.050 --> 01:16:27.339
Can I get you anything?

916
01:16:27.419 --> 01:16:30.389
No. No, I'm fine, thank you.

917
01:16:31.556 --> 01:16:33.345
How can I help?

918
01:16:33.425 --> 01:16:35.094
There seems to be more
to Gordon's death.

919
01:16:36.127 --> 01:16:38.550
Hmm, in what way?

920
01:16:38.630 --> 01:16:42.020
When I saw him that night
in... in the office,

921
01:16:42.100 --> 01:16:44.756
he had the Rememory
machine with him,

922
01:16:44.836 --> 01:16:48.960
but in the morning, and I was
one of the first ones there,

923
01:16:49.040 --> 01:16:51.596
it was gone, his
office ransacked.

924
01:16:51.676 --> 01:16:52.778
Someone took it.

925
01:16:55.146 --> 01:16:57.936
Are you saying someone
killed him for the machine?

926
01:16:58.016 --> 01:17:00.085
It rather would appear so.

927
01:17:01.586 --> 01:17:03.608
- Tent's all set up.

928
01:17:03.688 --> 01:17:05.090
Let's start a fire.

929
01:17:07.592 --> 01:17:08.980
Why haven't you told anyone?

930
01:17:09.060 --> 01:17:10.715
We need to keep this quiet.

931
01:17:10.795 --> 01:17:13.551
Word cannot get out that
the machine is missing.

932
01:17:13.631 --> 01:17:16.654
- Robert, you can't just not tell...
- Carolyn.

933
01:17:16.734 --> 01:17:18.137
I know who did this.

934
01:17:20.773 --> 01:17:23.610
Do you know who this is?

935
01:17:29.014 --> 01:17:31.837
- No.
- His name is Samuel Bloom.

936
01:17:31.917 --> 01:17:34.740
No, he doesn't use it much.

937
01:17:34.820 --> 01:17:36.809
He has the machine,

938
01:17:36.889 --> 01:17:41.547
and we believe he's the one
responsible for Gordon's death.

939
01:17:41.627 --> 01:17:43.482
If you see him, or
he tries to get

940
01:17:43.562 --> 01:17:45.117
in contact with you in any way,

941
01:17:45.197 --> 01:17:48.687
it's crucial that you let
me know immediately.

942
01:17:48.767 --> 01:17:50.089
As you know, the...

943
01:17:50.169 --> 01:17:52.772
the police have been
useless in this matter...

944
01:17:54.807 --> 01:17:56.643
but Cortex won't be.

945
01:17:57.710 --> 01:17:59.646
- Sure.
- Good.

946
01:18:02.014 --> 01:18:05.871
Carolyn, it's important...

947
01:18:05.951 --> 01:18:07.973
that word of this
doesn't get out

948
01:18:08.053 --> 01:18:11.744
- for Gordon's work.
- Of course.

949
01:18:11.824 --> 01:18:15.748
I know that he and I have had our
differences in our approach,

950
01:18:15.828 --> 01:18:19.819
but you must know that
I believed in him,

951
01:18:19.899 --> 01:18:23.889
and I only want his
creation to be his legacy.

952
01:18:23.969 --> 01:18:26.526
You want that, don't you?

953
01:18:26.606 --> 01:18:31.465
I would imagine that a man
so obsessed with memory

954
01:18:31.545 --> 01:18:33.900
would want people
to remember him,

955
01:18:33.980 --> 01:18:35.716
more than his invention.

956
01:18:37.017 --> 01:18:39.253
Yes, of course.

957
01:18:45.325 --> 01:18:47.094
You know, I would...

958
01:18:48.795 --> 01:18:53.167
I would hate it if I found
out that you were lying.

959
01:18:55.969 --> 01:18:57.738
I think you should leave.

960
01:18:59.205 --> 01:19:00.574
Good evening.

961
01:19:15.722 --> 01:19:18.645
- Who are you?
- Carolyn, please.

962
01:19:18.725 --> 01:19:20.146
Tell me, or I'm
calling the police.

963
01:19:20.226 --> 01:19:22.816
I'm sorry I lied to you.
I had to.

964
01:19:22.896 --> 01:19:24.784
- Why?
- To help.

965
01:19:24.864 --> 01:19:26.520
By stealing the machine?
What's your name?

966
01:19:26.600 --> 01:19:29.723
What's your real name?

967
01:19:29.803 --> 01:19:31.658
- Samuel Bloom.
- Did you kill Gordon?

968
01:19:31.738 --> 01:19:34.929
- No.
- Did you even know him?

969
01:19:35.009 --> 01:19:36.797
Did you meet at the hotel?

970
01:19:36.877 --> 01:19:39.748
Was any of that true, the
story that you told me?

971
01:19:43.150 --> 01:19:44.853
He told me about Jane.

972
01:19:46.654 --> 01:19:48.790
How he blamed himself, and...

973
01:19:50.658 --> 01:19:52.193
how much he missed her...

974
01:19:53.327 --> 01:19:55.049
that she wanted
to go to Scotland

975
01:19:55.129 --> 01:19:57.084
to see the real castles,

976
01:19:57.164 --> 01:20:01.689
and you were planning
on taking her.

977
01:20:01.769 --> 01:20:03.958
You were going to surprise
her for her tenth birthday.

978
01:20:04.038 --> 01:20:09.697
That he loved you more than
anything, and he just...

979
01:20:09.777 --> 01:20:11.599
He couldn't go on without you,

980
01:20:11.679 --> 01:20:16.270
but there were times when you
reminded him so much of Jane

981
01:20:16.350 --> 01:20:19.574
that he just couldn't bear it,

982
01:20:19.654 --> 01:20:22.276
that he was working on
something revolutionary

983
01:20:22.356 --> 01:20:25.694
that would allow him
to be with her again.

984
01:20:32.033 --> 01:20:33.888
I need your help, Carolyn.

985
01:20:33.968 --> 01:20:38.306
There was a woman, part of
the test group, Wendy Polk.

986
01:20:40.342 --> 01:20:41.677
That's her.

987
01:20:42.377 --> 01:20:44.032
She lied to me.

988
01:20:44.112 --> 01:20:47.603
I know now that she was the last
person to see Gordon alive.

989
01:20:47.683 --> 01:20:50.138
We need to get her
on this machine.

990
01:20:50.218 --> 01:20:52.688
We need to see what she saw.

991
01:20:54.356 --> 01:20:56.258
I'm gonna need your
help to do that.

992
01:21:01.430 --> 01:21:03.232
I want to try it.

993
01:21:24.252 --> 01:21:27.723
- You ready?
- Mm. No.

994
01:21:46.308 --> 01:21:48.277
My beauty.

995
01:21:59.021 --> 01:22:02.345
- You have made a commitment to me.
- It will free us up.

996
01:22:02.425 --> 01:22:05.195
You have made a
commitment to me.

997
01:22:11.768 --> 01:22:14.304
Can I put the star
on top of the tree?

998
01:22:43.266 --> 01:22:44.367
Look, Mommy!

999
01:22:46.068 --> 01:22:47.068
Beautiful.

1000
01:24:47.592 --> 01:24:50.195
I know you were the
last one who saw him.

1001
01:24:51.462 --> 01:24:53.932
- And I know you killed him.
- No.

1002
01:24:55.600 --> 01:24:56.855
I loved him.

1003
01:24:56.935 --> 01:24:58.323
Oh, is that right?

1004
01:24:58.403 --> 01:25:02.427
I hated him the day he died,
but I was in love with him.

1005
01:25:02.507 --> 01:25:05.296
All you were to him
was a distraction.

1006
01:25:05.376 --> 01:25:08.467
I know more than you think.

1007
01:25:08.547 --> 01:25:11.050
I know a part of his
life that you don't.

1008
01:25:11.951 --> 01:25:14.139
I still believed in him.

1009
01:25:14.219 --> 01:25:16.909
I also know memories he had
that you don't know about,

1010
01:25:16.989 --> 01:25:18.477
that he only shared with me.

1011
01:25:18.557 --> 01:25:20.412
I know a whole man you didn't.

1012
01:25:20.492 --> 01:25:24.083
Wow.

1013
01:25:24.163 --> 01:25:27.267
You're quite a confident
little shit, aren't you?

1014
01:25:28.734 --> 01:25:30.956
But he chose you, so...

1015
01:25:31.036 --> 01:25:33.392
We can't even share our stories,

1016
01:25:33.472 --> 01:25:36.242
and now I am left with nothing.

1017
01:25:37.743 --> 01:25:40.432
Well, we're both
left with nothing.

1018
01:25:40.512 --> 01:25:45.571
I just... I want all
this to be over.

1019
01:25:45.651 --> 01:25:47.506
I want my memories back.

1020
01:25:47.586 --> 01:25:50.690
Well, if you want
your memories back...

1021
01:25:51.991 --> 01:25:54.027
I'd like you to do
something for me.

1022
01:26:15.749 --> 01:26:19.152
Gordon? Oh, Jesus Christ!

1023
01:26:24.424 --> 01:26:28.315
Gordon? Gordon, wake up!

1024
01:26:28.395 --> 01:26:30.150
Please wake up!

1025
01:26:30.230 --> 01:26:33.153
Please wake up. Please,
please, please wake up.

1026
01:26:33.233 --> 01:26:34.233
